              {
                "text": "The rule: replace every 6 with 2. But the natural language says \"two colors, one of which is orange\" - orange is 7, magenta/light red is 6? Actually in ARC, 7 is orange, 6 is magenta. The rule says turn the not-orange color into light red. Hmm, \"light red\" = 2 (red). So orange=7 stays, the other color 6 becomes 2.\n\nSo I need to create 3 demonstration pairs where input has exactly two colors, one being orange (7), and the other being some color? The verifier only replaces 6 with 2. So the \"not-orange color\" must be 6. Wait but the verifier replaces SIX with TWO regardless.\n\nTo be safe, I should make inputs containing 6 and 7 (orange). Actually the verifier just replaces 6\u21922. The student must infer \"replace 6 with 2\" or \"turn non-orange into light red\". If I use different non-orange colors, the verifier wouldn't change them, giving inconsistent behavior. So all my examples should have 6 as the non-orange color.\n\nLet me create 3 diverse grids with colors 6 and 7 only (or with 0 background?). The rule says \"two colors, one of which is orange\". To be faithful, use exactly two colors: 7 (orange) and 6. The verifier replaces 6 with 2.\n\nLet me make grids of varying sizes.\n\nExample 1: 4x4 with mix.\nExample 2: 3x5.\nExample 3: 5x3.\n\nLet me just make them contain 6 and 7.\n\nGrid A:\n[[7,6,7],\n [6,6,6],\n [7,7,6]]\n\nGrid B:\n[[6,7,6,7],\n [7,6,7,6]]\n\nGrid C:\n[[7,7,7],\n [7,6,7],\n [7,7,7],\n [6,6,7]]\n\nThese all only contain 6 and 7. Output replaces 6\u21922.\n\nLet me call show_transformed_input for each.",
                "type": "reasoning_text"
              }
